### Optimality argument

- R0, RN, R1, and R2 each replace a subproblem by an equivalent one plus a
  constant, so they preserve the global minimum and record enough information
  for reconstruction.
- R∞ is exact: an all-`Inf` edge has no feasible assignment; a single finite
  edge forces both endpoint choices.
- On the remaining core, branch-and-bound enumerates all finite options. Its
  lower bound is a sum of per-node minima and per-edge minima, which can never
  exceed any completion’s true cost, including with negative benefit edges.
- At termination, `Solve` evaluates the reconstructed assignment directly and
  rejects any mismatch. Therefore every reported finite cost is both feasible
  and globally optimal; infeasible instances return `ErrUnsolvable`.
